== seed doc strings
package strings // import "strings"

Package strings has the text helpers that are not built-in methods of
string: repeating, padding, searching from the end, splitting into
fields, and classifying bytes.

FUNCTIONS

fn repeat(string s, int count) string
    `s` repeated `count` times. Crashes if `count` is negative.

fn padLeft(string s, int width, byte fill) string
    `s` with `fill` added in front until it is `width` characters long,
    counted as UTF-8 characters (the way `s.chars()` counts them: an invalid
    byte is one character). Each `fill` byte counts as one character.

fn padRight(string s, int width, byte fill) string
    The same as padLeft, adding at the end.

fn lastIndex(string s, string sub) int?
    The byte index of the last occurrence of `sub` in `s`, or null. An empty
    `sub` gives `s.len()`.

fn fields(string s) []string
    `s` split around runs of spaces, tabs, \n, \r, \v and \f, with no empty
    strings.

fn isDigit(byte b) bool
    '0' to '9'.

fn isLetter(byte b) bool
    ASCII 'a' to 'z' and 'A' to 'Z'.

fn isSpace(byte b) bool
    Space, \t, \n, \r, \v or \f.

-- exit 0
